"K. Bailey" schrieb:
> 
> nethack.addict.hojita@neverbox.com (Hojita Discordia) wrote in message news:<e97fcb1a.0308061052.48219516@posting.google.com>...
> > denisdavidvalente@hotmail.com (Denis) wrote:
> 
> > I'd say balancing the artifacts would make them more interesting.  If
> > I had to arbitrarily change (non-quest) artifacts to make some more
> > appealing (and others less appealing), here are some ideas:
> 
> Idea:
> *banes can be #invoked to cause fear/confusion in all monsters in
> sight.  (Come to think of it, fear and confusion might make good
> wizard spells).

At least confuse monster certainly is, esp. when you have enchantment
spells up to "skilled". Haven't gotten the hang of actually using
cause fear so far.

As far as the invocation idea goes: causing confusion in everything
nearby would be _very_ powerful, rather like short-term conflict.
Cause fear in all monsters susceptible to the slay only? Hmm, wouldn't
be much use, and having a jabberwock get scared by the sight of a
troll-slaying weapon wouldn't make much sense IMO (and if you exclude
demonbane and dragonbane, only werebane and trollsbane will be left).

> Were the powers of artifacts mostly set before weapon skills?

Mostly, yes. The post-skills changes were the elemental weapons
destroying stuff with their attacks, Sunsword gaining permanent light
and Werebane getting some protective abilities, IIRC.

> I think
> weapon skills tend to increase the power of the mainstream weapon
> types and decrease the power of the fringe ones, especially those
> with highly specific use like Trollsbane.  I think the damage of most
> banes should be increased to x3 at least, since most people using
> them will be doing so at a -4/-2 or after burning a skill slot.

I think triple damage against the chosen creature would be a good
idea; it'd make a more significant difference against such critters.

> > Grayswandir: 1.5x damage instead of double
> 
> Bringing down the top doesn't seem right for some reason.
> I think grayswandir could use a slight and subtle nerfing, but
> that's a different thread...

Grayswandir is so powerful because it does its extra damage against
everything _and_ scales up in power when enchanted (Excalibur has the
same artefact bonus, regardless of enchantment, Fire Brand doesn't do
its extra damage against everything). 

Perhaps if Grayswandir only doubled the _enchantment_-granted part of
its damage? This would make enchantment very important for it, and at
max. enchantment it'd have a higher extra damage than Excalibur's
average. But it wouldn't be like now where it's nearly equal to, say,
Frost Brand against non-cold-resistant stuff and almost twice as good
against cold-resistant critters.

> > Trollsbane: kills trolls instantly (or trolls hit/killed by it don't
> > regen?)

I'm not much in favour of instant-killing. Vorpal Blade performs
instant kills only one time in twenty (its 100% beheading chance
against Jabberwocks is a literary reference), and only against things
with a head. The Tsurugi has a 1/20 chance of an instant kill against
things that are no more than human sized (does double damage against
larger things) or an engulfing creature. It's a special power and
shouldn't be thrown around like confetti, esp. not in the shape of
100% instant-kill chances against whole cohorts of monsters.

> I like no ressies like everyone else. I think if it kills them they should
> leave no corpse, either, with some satisfactory "the troll disappears
> into noxious fumes" type message.

This might actually be the easiest way to achieve the no-resurrection
effect.

> > Dragonsbane: kills dragons instantly (or dragons always leave scales?)
> 
> Both of these seem yucky. I think it might simply be really good at
> killing them.  Perhaps make it a silver spear.

No - a lance! And indestructible, please. And some sort of protection
against dragon breath would be very nice, too - either a much higher
chance to be missed by dragon breath, or it grants resistance to fire,
acid and poison[1]. The protection should also extend to a steed while
riding.

[1] seems the best approximation of the legendary dragons' arsenal.

> > Werebane: werefoo cannot summon help when wielded
> 
> Immunity to lycanthropy?   Perhaps it should also force them
> back into human form when it hits them.

Some of this already exists. Having it work like a full ring of
protection from shape changers against were-animals only sounds good
to me. 

Additionally, it might render you immune to effects of the moon - as
long as you wield it, the effects of a full or new moon are
neutralised for you.

> > Ogresmasher: kills ogres instantly
> 
> Probably too strong, but on the other hand, sure why not.

I think any extras against ogres are utterly insignificant. Even
barbarians don't need any help against ogres, and they're the only
ones who are likely to meet significant numbers of them. I only vote
for no instant kills because i don't want too much instakilling. 

You're considered a carnivore while wielding it and don't suffer
cannibalism penalties; a Strength bonus sounds nice, but i fear it'd
be too good. But let's face it: it really needs another base weapon
than war hammer. Hmmm ... mace? Flail? If it granted 18/** Strength, a
war hammer would be enough, though.

> > Demonbane: does 2X damage against riders, as well (or more??)
> 
> 2x vs. riders, 3x versus other &s?

Triple damage against all demons. I don't think the game considers the
Riders to be demons.
As a nice extra, demons in your vicinity shouldn't be able to gate in
other demons as long as demonbane is wielded. Wouldn't prevent demons
being summoned by the "summon nasties" spell, or calling in a demon
lord/prince, though.

> > Grimtooth (I forget this even exists sometimes): always poisoned
> 
> (Not sure what poisoning does to monsters...)

Something like 1d6 extra damage _and_ a 1/10 chance of instant death,
unless the monster is poison-resistant. Nasty ... i like the idea.

> > Orcrist: 3X damage to orcs instead
> 
> Seems a good candidate for invoking for fear, although I'm not
> sure orcs are ever a problem by the time most people get their
> first artifact.
> 
> Perhaps give it bonuses in the hands of an orc.

Ummm... why? Orcrist is an anti-orc weapon. Maybe in the hands of an
elf, but that would only be justified if _all_ elven weapons had a
slight bonus for elves (or do they already?).

Always causes fear in orcs would fit the stories, no need for
invoking. The orcs would always run as soon as you got near, but that
won't stop them from firing while they run ...

> > [Giantslayer]
> 
> Make it a two-handed sword?

Oh, someone remembered this <g>. Making _any_ artefact two-handed
would make it extremely undesirable in the current incarnation of
NetHack. Heh, how about making Grayswandir a two-handed sword to
balance it? <cackle>

'nother idea: as long as Giantslayer is worn, you can catch/pick up
and hurl boulders. 

